# 2026-08-23 Windows gate-battery inventory (target/ai/windows-inventory-
# 2026-08-23.md on feat/windows-gate-inventory) found nine of these gates
# FAILING instead of reasoned-skipping on an unrunnable target -- eight by
# trying to build or exec a Linux-only ELF/syscall path and getting `Exec
# format error'/exit 127/126, and `nelisp-runtime-image-cache-gate' by a
# bare `[ -x $NELISP ]' test misreading a built-but-foreign-architecture
# binary as simply missing.  All nine now ask
# `nelisp-standalone--target-runnable-on-host-p' (scripts/nelisp-
# standalone-build.el) FIRST, the same convention `standalone-reader-test'
# already used, and print a `GATE-SKIP' line instead: nelisp-runtime-
# image-cache-gate, nelisp-source-command-substrate-gate, standalone-
# chunk-growth-test, standalone-parallel-compile-test, standalone-
# selfhost-mt-test, standalone-reader-smokes (checked once for the whole
# 34-smoke batch, since every sub-smoke execs the same binary),
# nelisp-nelix-native-hot-gate (see its own note below), and the nl-
# condition/nl-safe/nl-resource/standalone-reader-buffer/nl-actor smokes
# further down.  `nelisp-source-command-substrate-gate' also stopped using
# `mktemp -d' (which defaults into `/tmp', a namespace Git Bash and native
# Windows Emacs read differently) for the temp paths it hands to a
# separate Emacs `--eval'; it now uses a repo-local `target/tmp.XXXXXX'
# dir, which resolves identically from both sides.  `standalone-reader-
# smokes' also moved its per-smoke logs from `/tmp' to `target/tmp/' for
# the same reason (MSYS2 `make' and Git Bash `tail' disagreed about where
# `/tmp' lived in the same inventory run).  Verified on Linux (this
# session): mocked unrunnable via `NELISP_STANDALONE_TARGET=windows-
# x86_64', each of the nine prints its `GATE-SKIP' line and exits 0; with
# the real linux-x86_64 target, `standalone-reader-smokes' still reports
# `GATE-COUNT checked=34 findings=0'. Not Windows-verified -- the owner's
# next runbook run on Windows is the actual proof.
nelisp-runtime-image-cache-gate
nelisp-source-command-substrate-gate
standalone-chunk-growth-test
# precise-root-coverage (2026-08-29): the mid-form collector marks from the
# precise recorded-frame arms AND a conservative native-stack scan, and the
# scan is broad enough to hide the deletion of a precise arm entirely -- the
# whole reader-smoke suite stays green with `nl_gc_mark_recorded_frame's
# `result' arm removed.  This gate runs a collection-driving workload with the
# scan off, where that same deletion is an immediate SIGSEGV, and additionally
# pins that suppressing the parse-pool slot walk changes nothing.  Its scope is
# one synthetic workload on purpose: the precise surface does NOT carry a real
# module load (the anvil bootstrap segfaults in under a second with the scan
# off, on a clean tree), so this is not a claim that the scan is removable.
precise-root-coverage
