# chan-1', the one piece with genuinely new protocol logic over the
# shipped `nelisp-make-chan') and `nl-clj-go' itself both survive the
# same standalone substrate `nelisp-actor' does, across the exchange's
# several round trips.  Makes exactly ONE call into the baked demo --
# a real, measured, not-yet-root-caused defect this session found: a
# SECOND top-level call to the same baked function in one process
# hangs (low, non-spinning CPU across a 60s wait -- genuinely blocked,
# not a busy loop), even though a single call completes correctly
# every time this session tested it; prime suspect named, not chased
# further, in the smoke file's own Commentary (`nelisp-actor--reset'
# resets the id counter, so a second call's actors reuse a first
# call's ids while that first call's channel mediators are still
# alive, by this package's own deliberate immortal-mediator design).
# A second, separately-named gap the smoke does NOT paper over: the
# demo exchange itself uses `nelisp-chan-send'/`nelisp-chan-recv'
# directly, not this package's own `nl-clj-<!'/`nl-clj->!' -- those are
# fully correct on host Emacs (`nl-clj-async-test.el', 29/29 green,
# same producer/consumer and ping/pong shapes) but were separately
# measured to hang standalone under repeated resumption from inside a
# `while' loop, also not root-caused further; see `examples/nl-clj-
# async/go-ping-pong.el's own Commentary for the fullest account of
# both.  Listed optional (not promoted to required, no tools/gate-
# mutations.txt row yet, not wired into `nelisp-ai.sh extras'): this
# package's own next landing after `nl-clj-standalone-smoke' above,
# following that same "just shipped, not yet promoted" precedent --
# promote once BOTH open gaps above are closed (or the smoke's own
# scope is re-narrowed to only what is proven), or sooner if a real
# defect surfaces here first.
nl-clj-async-standalone-smoke?
